-- =============================================================================
-- A multi-clause group is compiled to local definitions holding its later
-- clauses. Until 2026-10-07 they were named `__pm_fallthrough_<k>`, a name a
-- drafter can spell too; now no source can spell their names. A definition
-- the drafter calls `__pm_fallthrough_0` is the drafter's own, and every
-- clause body that names it reads it (review of legalese/l4-ide#545).
--
-- Here the clauses after the first one can never run, so their binding is
-- checked and then dropped; the first clause's body reads the drafter's
-- `__pm_fallthrough_0`, and answers 7 (probe h02). An earlier build let it
-- read the dropped binding instead, and failed with an internal error when it
-- ran.
-- =============================================================================
DECLARE Colour IS ONE OF Red, Green, Blue

`__pm_fallthrough_0` MEANS 7

GIVEN c IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `refs user` c   IS `__pm_fallthrough_0`
DECIDE `refs user` Red IS 2

#EVAL `refs user` Green -- 7
#EVAL `refs user` Red   -- 7: the first clause matches every input

-- Here the clause after the first can run, and the first clause's body still
-- reads the drafter's `__pm_fallthrough_0`. A build that gave its own binding
-- of the later clauses that name read the binding instead: `refs user live`
-- Red then found no clause for Red, though the first clause is for Red (probe
-- userft).
GIVEN c IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `refs user live` Red   IS `__pm_fallthrough_0`
DECIDE `refs user live` Green IS 2
DECIDE `refs user live` Blue  IS 3

#EVAL `refs user live` Red   -- 7
#EVAL `refs user live` Green -- 2
